    "q-324458": {
      "dom_id": "step-q324458",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "In electronics manufacturing, the average resistance of a certain type of resistor is $100\\,Ω.$ However, $5%$ of resistors have resistance no more than $96.5\\,Ω.$ Assuming that resistor resistances are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the resistance, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.25$ | $0.20$ | $0.15$ | $0.10$ | $0.05$ | $0.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$Ω$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the resistance (in ohms). Then we have $X∼N(100,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 96.5)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 96.5) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{96.5-100}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.5}{1.6449} \\\\  & ≈2.13\\,Ω.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
